matics at Trinity College, Cambridge, where his tutor was John Edensor Littlewood (they were not related). ... * The skeleton key of mathematics: a simple account of complex algebraic theories, Hutchison & Company, London, 1949; 2nd edition Harper & Brothers, New York, 1960; 2002 Dover pbk reprint; 2013 Dover ebook edition ... Book Review: A university algebra.\nDudley E. Littlewood\nBritish mathematician\n\nDudley Ernest Littlewood (7 September 1903, London –\n6 October 1979, Llandudno) was a British mathematician known for his work in group representation theory.\n\nHe read mathematics at Trinity College, Cambridge, where his tutor was John Edensor Littlewood (they were not related). He was a lecturer at University College, Swansea from 1928 to 1947, and in 1948 took up the chair of mathematics at University College of North Wales, Bangor, retiring in 1970.\n\nHe worked on invariant theory and group representation theory, especially of the symmetric group, often in collaboration with Archibald Read Richardson of Swansea. They introduced the immanant of a matrix, studied Schur functions and developed the Littlewood-Richardson rule for their multiplication. Littlewood was also interested in the application of representation theory to quantum mechanics.\n\n## Selected publications\n\n* The theory of group characters and matrix representations of groups. 1940; 2nd edition Oxford 1950.\n* The skeleton key of mathematics: a simple account of complex algebraic theories, Hutchison & Company, London, 1949; 2nd edition Harper & Brothers, New York, 1960; 2002 Dover pbk reprint; 2013 Dover ebook edition\n* A university algebra. (1950); 2nd edition 1961.\n\n- Albert, A. A. Review of The theory of group characters and matrix representations by D. E. Littlewood. Bull. Amer. Math. Soc.\n- Turnbull, H. W. Review of The Skeleton Key of Mathematics by D. E. Littlewood. The Mathematical Gazette.\n- Daniel Zelinsky. Book Review: A university algebra. Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society.\n\n## See also\n\n* Hall-Littlewood polynomials\n* Plethysm\n* Restricted representation\n\n## References\n\n## External links\n\n* \n* \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories | Mathematical Association of America (https://old.maa.org/press/maa-reviews/the-skeleton-key-of-mathematics-a-simple-account-of-complex-algebraic-theories)\nciteturn658search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.4 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; LittlewoodPublisher:Dover Publications ... The skeleton key is an analogy and is explained more clearly in the body as a master key: abstraction in mathematics gives us a way to unlock many problems, just as a master key allows us to unlock many locks, by embodying what they have in common. ... The present book is pitched low and was probably a popular-math book when it came out, while the Guide books are generally at a graduate level.\n\n# The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\n\nImage\n\n###### \n\nDudley E. Littlewood\n\nPublisher: \n\nDover Publications\n\nPublication Date: \n\n2002\n\nNumber of Pages: \n\n144\n\nFormat: \n\nPaperback\n\nPrice: \n\n9.95\n\nISBN: \n\n0486425436\n\nCategory: \n\nGeneral\n\n  * MAA Review\n  * Table of Contents\n\n[Reviewed by\n\nAllen Stenger\n\n, on\n\n05/27/2015\n\n]\n\nThis is a dated survey of algebraic structures. Despite the title, it does not cover all of mathematics, but just abstract algebra. The skeleton key is an analogy and is explained more clearly in the body as a master key: abstraction in mathematics gives us a way to unlock many problems, just as a master key allows us to unlock many locks, by embodying what they have in common. The present work is a Dover 2002 unaltered reprint of the 1949 Hutchinson & Company edition.\n\nTypically each chapter covers one structure, such as Groups. The chapter gives a strong motivation for the structure (this is the best part of the book), defines it, gives some examples, and outlines a few results. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories - Dudley Ernest Littlewood - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books?id=5fjuAAAAMAAJ)\nciteturn658search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 66.8 years ago; Crawled: last week; # The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic TheoriesImage: Front Cover Dudley Ernest Littlewood Harper & Brothers, 1960 - Algebra - 138 pages### From inside the book ... Publisher  | Harper & Brothers, 1960\n\n# The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\n\nImage: Front CoverDudley Ernest LittlewoodHarper & Brothers, 1960 - Algebra - 138 pages\n\n### From inside the book\n\n### Contents\n\nPreface Page  | 7\n | \nNumbers  | 15\n | \n\nComplex Numbers and Algebraic  | 44\n | \n\n5 other sections not shown\n\n### Other editions - View all\n\n | The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\nD. E. Littlewood\nLimited preview - 2013\n | \n\n | The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\nDudley Ernest Littlewood\nLimited preview - 2002\n | \n\n | The Skeleton Key of Mathematics: A Simple Account of Complex Algebraic Theories\nDudley Ernest Littlewood\nSnippet view - 1949\n\n### Common terms and phrases\n\na₁ Aijk called co-ordinates coefficients commutative complex numbers concomitants congruence continued fraction corresponding cubic cycle of order defined denoted division algebra equation of degree equivalent Euclid's Algorithm expressed in terms factor of zero form a field fraction geometry gives ground form group algebra group of order Hence homogeneous co-ordinates idempotent Illus integral domain invariant irreducible linear mathematics matrix of transformation matrix representation metho